WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.600
Let us talk about calculus.

00:02.460 --> 00:05.380
I guess you all are familiar with the equation of a line.

00:05.980 --> 00:08.460
We all have studied it in our school days, right?

00:08.880 --> 00:11.800
We write it something like y equals x,

00:12.240 --> 00:15.500
where if we have this line, then at every point on the line,

00:15.820 --> 00:19.020
the value of y depends on the value of x.

00:19.520 --> 00:24.640
For example, if x equals 2, then y equals 2, right?

00:24.640 --> 00:29.300
This relationship shows how y changes as x changes.

00:30.000 --> 00:32.400
But this kind of relationship is easy to visualize

00:32.400 --> 00:36.840
because it's a straight line, constant and predictable.

00:37.560 --> 00:42.160
Now, imagine you're a curious thinker in the 1600s,

00:42.620 --> 00:46.380
when the world was just beginning to explore the motion of planets,

00:46.980 --> 00:51.660
the falling of objects, and how things move and change over time.

00:52.000 --> 00:54.580
Straight lines didn't cut it anymore.

00:55.400 --> 00:58.260
Things in nature didn't always behave predictably

00:58.260 --> 00:59.980
or move in straight lines.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
Think of a ball rolling down a hill, or water flowing into a basin.

01:06.000 --> 01:11.000
How do we even begin to describe such motion mathematically?

01:11.000 --> 01:14.000
Let's go back to the straight line for a moment.

01:14.000 --> 01:17.000
A straight line has a very special property.

01:17.000 --> 01:19.000
It has a constant slope.

01:19.000 --> 01:22.000
The slope tells us how steep the line is.

01:22.000 --> 01:26.000
That is how much y changes when x changes.

01:26.000 --> 01:30.000
For y equals x, the slope is constant.

01:30.000 --> 01:34.000
y equals 1, meaning for every one step forward in x,

01:34.000 --> 01:37.000
y goes up by 1, right?

01:37.000 --> 01:42.000
Now consider another line, y equals 2 times x.

01:42.000 --> 01:47.000
The slope of this line is 2, meaning that for every one step forward in x,

01:47.000 --> 01:50.000
y now goes up by 2.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.000
In other words, this line is much steeper than the first one.

01:54.000 --> 01:58.000
Therefore, slope is like a measure of the line's steepness.

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
It tells us how fast y is changing compared to x.

02:02.000 --> 02:06.000
Now, imagine a situation where the slope is 0.

02:06.000 --> 02:10.000
For instance, if you have a line like y equals 3,

02:10.000 --> 02:12.000
a horizontal line,

02:12.000 --> 02:15.000
then we know that the slope of this line is 0,

02:15.000 --> 02:18.000
because y doesn't change at all,

02:18.000 --> 02:21.000
no matter how much x changes.

02:21.000 --> 02:25.000
On the other hand, if the slope is a very large number,

02:25.000 --> 02:27.000
the line is super steep,

02:27.000 --> 02:29.000
almost vertical.

02:29.000 --> 02:31.000
I think you understood my point.

02:31.000 --> 02:35.000
But then, someone like Galileo might come along and say,

02:35.000 --> 02:39.000
What about something like y equals x squared?

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
That's not a straight line.

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
It's a curve.

02:43.000 --> 02:47.000
For example, we all know that distance equals speed into time.

02:47.000 --> 02:51.000
Now assume you are running at a constant speed,

02:51.000 --> 02:53.000
say 2 meters per second.

02:53.000 --> 02:56.000
So d will now be equal to 2t,

02:56.000 --> 03:01.000
where d is the distance and t is the time, right?

03:01.000 --> 03:05.000
Now if I draw this graph where I put time on x-axis

03:05.000 --> 03:07.000
and distance on y-axis,

03:07.000 --> 03:09.000
then we get this straight line,

03:09.000 --> 03:14.000
and the slope of this line will be the same as your constant speed of 2.

03:14.000 --> 03:17.000
But now, assume you start running in a race,

03:17.000 --> 03:20.000
and instead of maintaining a constant speed,

03:20.000 --> 03:24.000
you begin slowly and then gradually speed up.

03:24.000 --> 03:25.000
At first,

03:25.000 --> 03:28.000
you might cover only 1 meter in the first second,

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
which is equal to this point,

03:30.000 --> 03:32.000
then 3 meters in the second second,

03:32.000 --> 03:35.000
and perhaps 6 meters in the third second.

03:35.000 --> 03:39.000
The distance you cover is no longer proportional to time,

03:39.000 --> 03:44.000
and the graph of distance versus time will no longer be a straight line.

03:44.000 --> 03:46.000
It will be a curve.

03:46.000 --> 03:47.000
In this case,

03:47.000 --> 03:49.000
your speed or the slope of the graph,

03:49.000 --> 03:51.000
or the steepness of the curve,

03:51.000 --> 03:53.000
keeps changing.

03:53.000 --> 03:54.000
At the start,

03:54.000 --> 03:58.000
your speed or the slope is small because you're running slowly.

03:58.000 --> 03:59.000
As you speed up,

03:59.000 --> 04:01.000
the slope becomes steeper,

04:01.000 --> 04:05.000
showing that the distance you cover in each second is increasing.

04:05.000 --> 04:08.000
Therefore, at any given moment,

04:08.000 --> 04:13.000
the slope of the curve represents your speed at that exact point in time.

04:13.000 --> 04:15.000
Back in the 1600s,

04:15.000 --> 04:17.000
this was a huge problem.

04:17.000 --> 04:19.000
The slope wasn't constant anymore.

04:19.000 --> 04:21.000
People could draw the curve,

04:21.000 --> 04:23.000
but they had no idea how to calculate the slope

04:23.000 --> 04:25.000
at any specific point on it.

04:25.000 --> 04:28.000
And then came geniuses like Isaac Newton

04:28.000 --> 04:31.000
and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz

04:31.000 --> 04:33.000
in the late 1600s.

04:33.000 --> 04:35.000
Both of them,

04:35.000 --> 04:36.000
working separately,

04:36.000 --> 04:39.000
came up with a groundbreaking idea.

04:39.000 --> 04:42.000
Instead of thinking about the whole curve at once,

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
they zoomed in really, really close,

04:45.000 --> 04:49.000
so close that the curve looked almost like a straight line.

04:49.000 --> 04:52.000
This is how the concept of the derivative was born.

04:52.000 --> 04:56.000
We all know that if we have two points like this on a straight line,

04:56.000 --> 05:05.000
then its slope is given as y2-y1 over x2-x1.

05:05.000 --> 05:09.000
Newton and Leibniz realized that to find the slope of a curve,

05:09.000 --> 05:13.000
you could look at two points getting closer and closer together,

05:13.000 --> 05:17.000
such that they could find the exact slope at a single point.

05:17.000 --> 05:19.000
But how does it work?

05:19.000 --> 05:21.000
Let us understand this with an example.

05:22.000 --> 05:26.000
Suppose we have a curve y equals x2.

05:26.000 --> 05:31.000
Now if we want to find the slope of this curve at say x equals 1,

05:31.000 --> 05:33.000
then assume we take two points

05:33.000 --> 05:35.000
where the first point is obviously 1

05:35.000 --> 05:38.000
and 1 square or 1 itself.

05:38.000 --> 05:41.000
And then let us take another very close point,

05:41.000 --> 05:44.000
say x equals 1.1

05:44.000 --> 05:48.000
and y equals 1.1 square or 1.21.

05:48.000 --> 05:51.000
Now these two points are so close to each other

05:51.000 --> 05:55.000
that the curve here behaves almost as a straight line.

05:55.000 --> 05:58.000
So what will be the value of this slope?

05:58.000 --> 06:02.000
It will be this minus this over 1.1 minus 1, right?

06:02.000 --> 06:06.000
This gives the value of slope at this point as 2.1.

06:06.000 --> 06:08.000
This is simply amazing.

06:08.000 --> 06:11.000
Now let us level up our gear a bit more.

06:11.000 --> 06:16.000
Assume we have a point as x and the y value as x square.

06:16.000 --> 06:19.000
And another point at some distance h from x,

06:19.000 --> 06:21.000
say x plus x.

06:21.000 --> 06:23.000
This will be x plus h,

06:23.000 --> 06:28.000
such that its y value will be x plus h whole square.

06:28.000 --> 06:31.000
So now what will be the slope of this line?

06:31.000 --> 06:38.000
It will be x plus h whole square minus x square over x plus h minus x.

06:38.000 --> 06:40.000
Look at the denominator.

06:40.000 --> 06:44.000
x will get cancelled out and we are only left with h.

06:44.000 --> 06:47.000
Now expand the numerator to get this as

06:47.000 --> 06:51.000
x square plus 2x times h plus h square

06:51.000 --> 06:53.000
and minus this x square.

06:53.000 --> 06:56.000
Oh look, now x square will get cancelled out

06:56.000 --> 07:01.000
and we are left with 2x times h plus h square over h.

07:01.000 --> 07:05.000
Divide it properly to remove h from the denominator

07:05.000 --> 07:09.000
and we are left with 2x plus h.

07:09.000 --> 07:11.000
Here comes the magic.

07:11.000 --> 07:14.000
We want both these points to be as close as possible,

07:14.000 --> 07:17.000
which means this h is so so small

07:17.000 --> 07:20.000
that it is almost invisible to our naked eye.

07:21.000 --> 07:24.000
Thus we say that h tends to zero.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.000
And since h is not exactly zero,

07:27.000 --> 07:29.000
we put this lim here,

07:29.000 --> 07:31.000
which stands for limit,

07:31.000 --> 07:33.000
which means what is the value of this thing

07:33.000 --> 07:36.000
when h is very close to zero.

07:36.000 --> 07:38.000
And to solve for it,

07:38.000 --> 07:40.000
we put h as zero,

07:40.000 --> 07:45.000
which gives us 2x as the slope of the curve y equals x square.

07:45.000 --> 07:48.000
So we say that the derivative of y,

07:48.000 --> 07:51.000
which is represented using dy over dx,

07:51.000 --> 07:56.000
which indicates the change in y with respect to x at a given point,

07:56.000 --> 08:00.000
is 2x when y equals x square.

08:00.000 --> 08:02.000
Now at x equals 1,

08:02.000 --> 08:07.000
what will be the derivative of y with respect to x?

08:07.000 --> 08:08.000
Or in other words,

08:08.000 --> 08:12.000
what will be the slope of this curve at x equals 1?

08:12.000 --> 08:17.000
Just substitute x as 1 here and we are done.

08:17.000 --> 08:21.000
The slope of this curve at x equals 1 is 2, right?

08:21.000 --> 08:26.000
We solved it previously and got our answer as 2.1, remember?

08:26.000 --> 08:28.000
Which was so close to 2,

08:28.000 --> 08:30.000
but not exactly 2,

08:30.000 --> 08:33.000
because the value of h was 0.1,

08:33.000 --> 08:37.000
which although small but was not very close to zero.

08:37.000 --> 08:39.000
Now to generalize this,

08:39.000 --> 08:41.000
instead of using x square,

08:41.000 --> 08:45.000
assume we denote y as a function of x or f of x.

08:45.000 --> 08:47.000
Now at this point x,

08:47.000 --> 08:50.000
we have y as f of x,

08:50.000 --> 08:53.000
at this point which is x plus h,

08:53.000 --> 08:55.000
we have y or the output

08:55.000 --> 09:00.000
when the input is x plus h as f of x plus h.

09:00.000 --> 09:02.000
So the slope of this line will be

09:02.000 --> 09:09.000
f of x plus h minus f of x over x plus h minus x, right?

09:09.000 --> 09:11.000
This will get cancelled out

09:11.000 --> 09:16.000
and we are left with f of x plus h minus f of x over h.

09:16.000 --> 09:19.000
Finally, we put a limit here which shows

09:19.000 --> 09:22.000
h tends to zero and that's it.

09:22.000 --> 09:26.000
This is the derivative of the function y or f of x

09:26.000 --> 09:28.000
with respect to x.

09:28.000 --> 09:31.000
Now using the method I have just shown you,

09:31.000 --> 09:34.000
mathematicians have already calculated derivatives

09:34.000 --> 09:37.000
of some famous functions like these.

09:41.000 --> 09:44.000
So our life has now become super easy.

09:44.000 --> 09:46.000
So using this formula,

09:46.000 --> 09:48.000
or deriving on your own,

09:48.000 --> 09:50.000
now can tell me in the comments

09:50.000 --> 09:57.000
what will be the derivative of the curve y equals x cube at x equals 2.

09:57.000 --> 10:00.000
This simple idea of zooming in on a curve

10:00.000 --> 10:02.000
and finding its slope

10:02.000 --> 10:04.000
has transformed how we understand

10:04.000 --> 10:07.000
and solve problems in the world around us.

10:07.000 --> 10:10.000
Suddenly, derivatives became a universal language

10:10.000 --> 10:12.000
for describing change

10:12.000 --> 10:14.000
and it is used in our day-to-day life,

10:14.000 --> 10:16.000
starting from measuring speed

10:16.000 --> 10:18.000
to predicting stock prices.

10:18.000 --> 10:21.000
Tracking heart rate changes in medicine

10:21.000 --> 10:24.000
and analyzing an athlete's speed during a race.

10:24.000 --> 10:26.000
They help in weather forecasting

10:26.000 --> 10:28.000
by predicting temperature changes,

10:28.000 --> 10:31.000
studying population growth in biology,

10:31.000 --> 10:33.000
answering questions like

10:33.000 --> 10:35.000
why planets orbit in ellipses

10:35.000 --> 10:39.000
and calculating marginal costs in economics.

10:39.000 --> 10:42.000
Derivatives also control robotic movements,

10:42.000 --> 10:44.000
optimize traffic flow,

10:44.000 --> 10:46.000
enhance sound engineering

10:46.000 --> 10:49.000
and even monitor the spread of diseases.

10:49.000 --> 10:52.000
Now if this video gets 3,000 likes,

10:52.000 --> 10:55.000
then I will bring another banger video on calculus,

10:55.000 --> 10:58.000
which will be much more interesting than this one.

10:58.000 --> 11:00.000
